{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And you shall not bring an abomination into your house.</b> From here the Sages, blessed be their memory, learned by way of asmakhta that it is forbidden to rent one's house to a gentile for a dwelling, because the gentile brings idolatry into it, and this Israelite who rents transgresses — for rental does not acquire the land for the tenant, and the land is not the gentile's but the Israelite's; therefore he transgresses through it. But we find some of the Geonim, of blessed memory, who wrote that this applies only in the land of Israel, because there is the principal place of idolatry, and so it appears in the Jerusalem Talmud, and so is the opinion of Rabbeinu Chananel, of blessed memory, and so we practice in this matter with a lenient ruling now. But the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote: a master of soul must abstain from renting even in the land of Ishmael." "<b>You shall utterly detest it.</b> Scripture teaches that a person is obligated to detest idolatry, to mention it in a derogatory expression. If its name was בית גליא, which is an expression of height, they would call it בית כריא, which is an expression of depth and lowness. And so they further said: all mockery is forbidden except mockery of idolatry, which is permitted, as it is said (Isaiah 46): ״כרע בל קרס בנו״, and it is written (ibid.): ״כרסו כרעו יחדו לא יכלו מלט משא״. And likewise the name of Mercolis — its name was Kolos among its worshippers, and that is an expression of praise and honor, and the Sages, of blessed memory, exchanged it for the name Mercolis: מר is an expression of exchange and substitution, as in the language of the Sages, of blessed memory: מר דשחוטה בחליפי שחוטה. And likewise they exchanged Kolos for Kolis, which is an expression of mockery and disgrace, from the expression (Psalms 79): לעג וקלס; so explained Rabbeinu Tam, of blessed memory. And so they further said: לפנים מן הקלקלון אסור, and Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: curtains that they make for idolatry and offer before it לפנים, and they make them for modesty and honor. But we read: לפנים מן הקלקלון אסור — in order to mention it by the name קלון, וכבודם בקלון אמיר. And there are those who read מרקלוס, that is, the exchange of Kolos, and it is mockery; and if so, the verse ״שקץ תשקצנו״ encompasses both deed and the mention of their name."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:44.221957103Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}